Hi Everett,
Are you asking for something to read through a file and fix up the indentat=
ions, or are you asking for an editor that will help you monitor the indent=
ations? If so, EdSharp does a great job of that, especially if you use the =
JAWS Settings Packager to import my sound scheme that I donated to it and t=
urn on the JAWS indentation. It plays a different piano note for every two =
spaces of indentation up to eight levels of indentation, not including the =
margin. Here are the relevant key strokes in EdSharp.

Say Indentation=3DAlt+I, Say the indentation level of the current line, or =
the preceding line with less indentation if repeated
Say Indentation=3DAlt+I, Say the indentation level of the current line, or =
the preceding line with less indentation if repeated
Format Code =3DControl+4, Arrange indentation and other stylistic conventio=
ns in a C-like language
Infer Indent=3DAlt+RightBracket, Infer the indent unit of the current docum=
ent, or configure EdSharp accordingly if repeated
Toggle Indentation=3DWindows+Grave, Toggle announcement of indentation by J=
AWS (turns on the sound scheme if you have it installed and are using it by=
 picking it in the configuration manager)
Indent Mode=3DAlt+Shift+I, Toggle auto indent with Enter, and announcement =
of indentation changes (Makes EdSharp speak indentation levels independentl=
y of the JAWS sound scheme)
Enter New Line=3DEnter, Start new line at left margin
Indent New Line=3DShift+Enter, Start new line with same indentation as curr=
ent one
Indent New Line Prior=3DAlt+Shift+Enter, insert prior line with same indent=
ation as current one
Indent=3DTab, Indent current line or selected text by one unit
Outdent=3DShift+Tab, Reduce indentation of current or selected lines by one=
 unit
Align=3DAlt+Shift+A, Adjust indentation of current or selected lines accord=
ing to prior line
Next Block=3DControl+B, Go to the next block of code, having the same or le=
ss indentation
Prior Block=3DControl+Shift+B, Go to the previous block of code, having the=
 same or less indentation
Next Indent=3DControl+I, Go to the next change in indentation
Prior Indent=3DControl+Shift+I, Go to the previous change in indentation
Right Brace=3DControl+Shift+RightBracket, Search forward for matching right=
 brace character
Left Brace=3DControl+Shift+LeftBracket, Search backward for matching left b=
race character
End Tag=3DControl+Shift+Period, go to closing tag of HTML element
Start Tag=3DControl+Shift+Comma, Go to opening tag of HTML element
Next Part=3DAlt+PageDown, Go to next match of NavigatePart setting (finds f=
unction, sub, class, o def, or whatever marks the beginning of that kind of=
 stuff for the language you're coding in)
Prior Part=3DAlt+PageUp, Go to previous match of NavigatePart setting (same=
 as above)
Go to Part=3DAlt+Shift+G, Pick a part to go to (pick from a list of routine=
s)

Jim

